  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Consider the equation ${y}^{\u2033}-6{y}^{\u2032}+10y=0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$",
  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{\u2033}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+10{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda -3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.",
